INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A factory''s costing team compares two CM (Cut-Make) quotes for the same style: Factory A quotes $2.20/piece with a stated 65% target line efficiency, Factory B quotes $1.95/piece with a stated 78% target line efficiency. If BOTH factories pay the SAME effective hourly wage rate per operator, what does the meaningfully HIGHER efficiency target at the LOWER-priced Factory B most likely indicate about the actual comparison?', '["This comparison has no meaningful interpretation; CM price and stated efficiency target are entirely unrelated numbers with zero connection to each other in factory costing","Since CM cost is fundamentally driven by (SAM × effective wage rate) ÷ efficiency%, a HIGHER efficiency ASSUMPTION directly justifies a LOWER quoted CM price for the SAME underlying SAM and wage rate — Factory B''s lower price may genuinely reflect superior real operational efficiency (a legitimate cost advantage) OR could reflect an OPTIMISTIC/unrealistic efficiency assumption used to make the quote look more competitive than actual achievable performance will support, meaning the buyer should verify Factory B''s CLAIMED 78% efficiency against its actual demonstrated track record before assuming the lower price is straightforwardly achievable","A higher stated efficiency target always means the SAME thing regardless of which factory states it, making any comparison or verification between the two factories'' claims unnecessary","CM price should always be evaluated in complete isolation from any stated efficiency assumption, since efficiency claims have no bearing on whether a quoted price is actually realistic or achievable"]'::jsonb, 'Since CM cost is fundamentally driven by (SAM × effective wage rate) ÷ efficiency%, a HIGHER efficiency ASSUMPTION directly justifies a LOWER quoted CM price for the SAME underlying SAM and wage rate — Factory B''s lower price may genuinely reflect superior real operational efficiency (a legitimate cost advantage) OR could reflect an OPTIMISTIC/unrealistic efficiency assumption used to make the quote look more competitive than actual achievable performance will support, meaning the buyer should verify Factory B''s CLAIMED 78% efficiency against its actual demonstrated track record before assuming the lower price is straightforwardly achievable', 'This directly extends the earlier CM-costing-sourcing-diligence discussion with the specific mathematical mechanism connecting efficiency assumptions to CM pricing — since CM cost is mathematically driven by (SAM × wage rate) ÷ efficiency% (the SAME underlying relationship established in the earlier operator-requirement-calculation discussion), a factory can quote a LOWER CM price simply by ASSUMING a higher efficiency% in its costing, regardless of whether that efficiency is actually achievable in real production — this means a buyer comparing quotes should specifically investigate whether Factory B''s stated 78% efficiency assumption is verified by its ACTUAL demonstrated track record (not just accepted at face value), since an unrealistically optimistic efficiency assumption could mean the quoted low price isn''t genuinely achievable once real production begins, directly echoing the earlier lesson about investigating WHY one factory quotes lower than another.', 3, '{"CM-quote-efficiency-assumption-verification"}'::text[]),
('44444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production planner calculates that a factory''s ''line balancing loss'' (the efficiency lost specifically from imperfect operator/machine allocation across operations, DISTINCT from other efficiency losses like absenteeism or machine breakdown) is currently 12 percentage points (i.e., theoretical maximum efficiency from perfect balancing would be 12 points higher than currently achieved). If the factory operates 8 lines and this 12-point loss is consistent across all lines, and each percentage point of efficiency is worth approximately $340/day in additional output value per line, what is the DAILY value of eliminating this SPECIFIC balancing loss across the WHOLE factory?', '["Daily value = 12 points × $340/point × 8 lines = $32,640/day — this substantial daily figure specifically isolates the value of the LINE BALANCING component alone (distinct from other efficiency-loss sources), directly justifying focused investment in industrial engineering/line-balancing expertise as a specific improvement priority","Daily value = 12×$340=$4,080, incorrectly omitting the 8-line multiplication and calculating the value for only a single line rather than the whole factory","Daily value = 12×8=96, incorrectly treating percentage points and line count as directly multipliable into a dollar figure without incorporating the actual $340/point value factor","Daily value cannot be calculated without knowing each individual operation''s specific SAM breakdown, making this factory-wide aggregate calculation technically impossible despite the given aggregate data"]'::jsonb, 'Daily value = 12 points × $340/point × 8 lines = $32,640/day — this substantial daily figure specifically isolates the value of the LINE BALANCING component alone (distinct from other efficiency-loss sources), directly justifying focused investment in industrial engineering/line-balancing expertise as a specific improvement priority', 'Daily value of eliminating this SPECIFIC efficiency-loss source = 12 percentage points × $340/point × 8 lines = $32,640/day — this connects to the earlier bottleneck/line-balancing discussions by showing the CONCRETE dollar value specifically attributable to line-balancing quality (isolated from other loss sources like absenteeism or machine breakdown), which is exactly the kind of specific, quantified business case that justifies dedicated investment in industrial engineering expertise or line-balancing software/methodology improvements, rather than treating ''efficiency improvement'' as one vague, undifferentiated goal without knowing which SPECIFIC loss source offers the largest quantified improvement opportunity.', 3, '{"line-balancing-loss-value-calculation"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
